    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "Hi @gmarkall, thanks for taking a look! I'm not sure I'm following though.\r\n\r\nDatashader relies on being able to initialize an array of NaN values. In psuedo-code\r\n\r\n```\r\na = [nan, nan, nan]\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen it repeatedly performs min/max operations (in parallel)\r\n\r\nFirst operation\r\n```\r\ncuda.atomic.max(a, 1, 3.0)\r\na\r\n```\r\n```\r\n[nan, 3.0, nan]\r\n```\r\nSecond operation\r\n```\r\ncuda.atomic.max(a, 1, 4.0)\r\na\r\n```\r\n```\r\n[nan, 4.0, nan]\r\n```\r\nI'm not sure what you mean by \"we'd like to fix this bug, and ensure that the old value is always returned\".\r\nDoes the new behavior of `nanmin`/`nanmax` you have in mind still behave this way?\r\n\r\nFWIW, there's already work in progress in Datashader to switch from `min`/`max` to `nanmin`/`nanmax` in https://github.com/holoviz/datashader/pull/940.\r\n\r\n",
