  {
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "Thanks for the quick reply @jonmmease! It does still behave that way - what you should also be able to do is:\r\n\r\n```python\r\na = [nan, nan, nan]\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python\r\nold = cuda.atomic.nanmax(a, 1, 3.0)\r\n```\r\n\r\nAt this point, `old`, should hold the value `nan` - but in the original implementation, it holds `3.0`. I think it looks like you're not using the returned value at all, so it should be safe for you if the `nanmax` and `nanmin` implementations always return the old value (i.e. `nan` in this case)?",
